Adaptive bit rate for CBR AAC and frames that fit one packet (Y2Linux).

BlueALSA keeps a three-packet Bluetooth socket buffer and blocks while the
radio cannot deliver; only LDAC adapts its rate. AAC kept its full CBR rate on
a weak link and the headphones ran dry. With --aac-abr the encoder steps down
(8/8, 6/8, 5/8, 4/8 of the rate, not below 128 kbit/s) when two packets are
queued or a write had to wait, and back up after ~10 s with an empty queue.
The configured CBR rate is also capped so a 1024-sample frame fits one packet
of the link's MTU: some headsets cannot reassemble fragmented AAC frames, the
reason BlueALSA defaults to 220 kbit/s. EncoderStats reports AAC bit rate,
ABR level and adjustments (patch 0001). Decisions: src/y2-aac-abr.h.

